<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nanda Bayin</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1535–1600</span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Nanda Bayin was king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-toungoo-empire">First Toungoo Empire</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burma">Burma</a> from 1581 to 1599, the son and successor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayinnaung">Bayinnaung</a>. He inherited the largest empire in the history of mainland <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southeast-asia">Southeast Asia</a> but faced revolts, and his repeated failed campaigns against Siam under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naresuan">Naresuan</a>, which won its independence in 1584, exhausted the country. In 1599 the forces of Toungoo and Arakan took his capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bago">Pegu</a>, and he was killed in 1600.</p></div>
